[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: csw1, csw2
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Core routing policy (default route/origination) on the core switches csw1 and csw2.  

Mechanism: End-user traffic leaves the access VLAN toward the distribution switch (dswa1). Because the campus IGP (OSPF) does not carry a 0.0.0.0/0, dswa1 has no route to the Internet and immediately returns ICMP network unreachable. This manifests as “every web page times out” and traceroute showing the first hop replying “!N”.

Localization: 
- From PC1, traceroute to 1.1.1.1 stops at 10.1.0.2 with “!N” (dswa1). 
- dswa1 routing table has no default route (ip route shows only internal prefixes). 
- csw1/csw2 also have no default route (ip route show default returns nothing) and their FRR OSPF configs lack “default-information originate” and do not redistribute a static default. 
- The WAN router r1 is healthy: it has a default via 203.0.113.1, can ping public IPs, IP forwarding is on, and NAT rules for 10.1.0.0/24 exist.

Root cause: Default route to r1 (and its advertisement) was removed/not configured on both core switches, so no 0.0.0.0/0 is present or originated into OSPF.

Corrective fix: 
- On csw1: add default route via 10.0.0.33 (r1) and either “router ospf … default-information originate always” or “redistribute static” to advertise it. 
- On csw2: add default route via 10.0.0.37 and the same OSPF default origination. 
Once the cores inject the default, dswa1 will forward to the cores, r1 will NAT, and Internet access for PC1 (and all VLANs) will be restored. 
=== END ===